Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 23:14:14 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 40/52] kernel: message pipe hand-over and more wait fixes
 (#4292)

* ci: run the latest VitaAutotests

* kernel/sysmem: take an alignment of 0 for every memory block type

An allocation with HAS_ALIGNMENT refused an alignment of 0 for the PHYCONT types, since the check
for those types came before the one for 0, and a game that asks for that, like Need for Speed,
crashed on the failed allocation. Like on a Vita, an alignment of 0 now asks for no alignment for
every type, and the block gets its type's smallest one. memblock_alignment_zero passes.

* kernel: end a sync object wait whose deadline already passed before queueing it

A wait whose deadline had already passed when it would queue was queued anyway and then timed out,
so a waker could still serve it in between and it ran the steps of a queued timeout. Like on a
Vita, such a wait now ends with WAIT_TIMEOUT before it is queued, which also covers the event
flag's own check for a timeout of 0.

* kernel: hand message pipe bytes over in the call that moves them

A message pipe call that couldn't finish woke one waiter whose request fit, and that waiter copied
the bytes once its thread ran. So a send reached only one waiting receiver, bytes sent to a waiting
receive sat in the pipe until it ran, a receive let in only one waiting sender, and a wait that
ended early reported a size of 0.

Now each waiter keeps how far its transfer got, and the call that makes bytes movable copies them
straight between the sender's and the receiver's pieces, serves every waiter it can in queue order
and writes each one's size as it moves bytes. A FULL receive that times out, is canceled or deleted
keeps the bytes it got and reports their size, and a waiter back from its callbacks carries on
where it was. A try call that fails in FULL mode or behind waiters of its own side writes no size,
and a blocking call of 0 bytes ends at once in both modes (msgpipe_zero_bytes).

The pipe now waits with wait_on() and serves with serve() like the other objects, so the queue's
wake to recheck wait goes away. msgpipe_wake_state is skipped, since whether the woken waiter
already ran when its status is read is a race.

* kernel: count the bytes of waiting senders in a FULL try receive

A FULL try receive only counted the bytes the pipe held, so it failed with MSG_PIPE_EMPTY when the
waiting senders could make up the rest. Like on a Vita, it now also counts what the waiting senders
still have to give, and takes their bytes. msgpipe_try_receive_senders passes.

* kernel: queue new message pipe waiters behind a head that moved part of its bytes

A waiter of higher priority went in front of the first one even when that one had already moved
part of its bytes, so the next send went to the newcomer and split the first one's message. Like on
a Vita, nothing now goes in front of such a head: the queue asks its entry whether it keeps its
place, and puts the newcomer right behind it. msgpipe_partial_head_order passes.

* kernel: wake only the first timer waiter to check the event again

Only the first waiter of a timer waits for its event, the others wait for their turn. A new event
time, a waiter that left and a waiter that took the event woke every waiter to check again, and
all but the first went straight back to waiting. Now only the first one is woken, and it wakes the
next when it leaves.

WaitQueue no longer needs notify_all(), so it only has wakes that end a wait.

* kernel: check a wait's arguments before running its pending callbacks

A wait with callbacks ran the thread's pending callbacks first thing, so a call with a bad mode,
pattern or count, or on an event flag another thread holds, ran them and then failed. Like on a
Vita, each sync object's wait now checks first and runs the pending callbacks only once the checks
passed, right before it takes, and an error leaves them pending. The owner's own mutex or write
lock, which takes or fails at once, runs none.

The object's lock is released only while a callback that can run now runs. A wait with none
pending keeps its lock throughout, so a condition wait that just let go of its mutex can't miss a
signal before it is queued. cb_wait_check_order passes.

(cherry picked from commit 2ce71cc306afa8045cce2bc60e059a974a3809d8)
